  "findings": "Across 18 (concurrency, replicate) measurements spanning offered concurrency from 2 (below the 5-connection capacity) to 30 (6x capacity), L (independently sampled, time-averaged outstanding-request count) and lambda*W (computed from independently measured throughput and mean latency) agreed within a mean absolute relative error of 4.3% -- consistent with Little's Law holding over these stable, closed-loop measurement windows, using one self-consistent system boundary (client-observed dispatch-to-response) for all three quantities. Offered concurrency grew 15x (2 to 30) while measured throughput grew only 2.7x (98.0 to 268.6 req/s) -- essentially flat, and close to the 250 req/s ceiling predicted directly from capacity/service-time (5 connections / 20ms), a genuine finite-capacity effect from transport.MaxConnsPerHost, not a simulated one. This is a measured association between increased in-flight work and constrained throughput/rising latency under a real, finite-capacity system boundary -- not a claim that FlashFlow's edge or origin behave like a textbook M/M/c queue in general; the finding is scoped specifically to the transport-layer connection limit exercised here."
